# Standards Review — investor-portfolio-hexagonal

**Reviewed against:** `template-microservice` (`portfolio-service`) hexagonal architecture template
**Scope:** `investor-portfolio-hexagonal/` — static review only, code not executed
**Date:** 2026-08-13

This document summarizes how closely the team's implementation follows the hexagonal
architecture standards established in `template-microservice`, what deviates, what needs
attention before this goes further, and what must not be done going forward.

---

## 1. Architecture & Structure

### ✅ Followed
- Correct layering: `domain → application (port/in, port/out, service) → adapter (in/web, out/persistence)`.
- `domain` package has zero framework dependencies (no Spring, no JPA, no Servlet imports) — matches the core rule of domain purity.
- Ports defined as interfaces in both directions (`application/port/in`, `application/port/out`), implemented by `*Service` classes and `*PersistenceAdapter` classes respectively.
- Package-private visibility used correctly on some adapter classes (e.g. `InvestorPersistenceAdapter`, `PersistenceMapper`).

### ❌ Not followed
- No architecture fitness test. `template-microservice` has `HexagonalArchitectureTest` (ArchUnit) in `bootstrap/src/test/.../architecture/` that fails the build if domain imports a framework type, if application depends on adapters, or if adapters depend on each other. Nothing enforces the layering here beyond convention — a future change can silently break it and nobody will know.
- No DTO boundary. `template-microservice` maps domain objects to `record`-based `*Request`/`*Response` DTOs at the web adapter (`*Response.of(domain)`). Here, domain-model classes from `domain/model/` are serialized directly as HTTP responses (e.g. `InvestorPortfolioResponse`, `FamilyMfPortfolioResponse`).
- JPA entities and domain models share simple class names in different packages (e.g. `adapter/out/persistence/entity/primary/User.java` vs `domain/model/User.java`, no `JpaEntity` suffix) — collision-prone, and diverges from the `*JpaEntity` naming convention in the reference.

### 🔧 Needs attention
- Add an ArchUnit (or equivalent) fitness test before this pattern is reused as a template for other teams — otherwise "hexagonal" is only true today, not guaranteed tomorrow.
- Decide, as a team, whether skipping the DTO layer is an accepted tradeoff (README justifies it as avoiding remapping ~200 fields) or technical debt to schedule.

