## 6. Configuration & Security

These are deploy-blocking and should be treated as more urgent than style/test gaps.

### ❌ Not followed / must fix before any shared or production deployment
- **Plaintext secrets committed**: `application-dev.properties` and `application-prod.properties` contain hardcoded DB credentials (e.g. `Mfbox@96860!$`, `Amfi@96860!$`) and a plaintext `jwt.secret-key`. If this repository has been pushed to any shared remote, these credentials must be treated as compromised and rotated.
- **Prod port collision**: `application-prod.properties` uses port 8080 — the same port the legacy sibling service `investor-portfolio-service` uses in production. This will conflict at deploy time. Self-reported in the README as an unresolved known issue.
- **Dev config not portable**: `application-dev.properties` references another developer's local absolute Windows paths (`D:/GitHub/themfbox-microservices/investor-portfolio-service/...`) for `pdfDir`/`fontDir` — will break on any other machine.
- **Dead configuration**: `eureka.client.*` properties are set in dev/prod properties, but there is no `spring-cloud`/Eureka dependency in `pom.xml`. This config currently does nothing and may confuse whoever debugs service discovery later.

### 🔧 Needs attention
- Move all secrets to environment variables (`${ENV_VAR}` pattern, as in the reference) or a secrets manager — never commit plaintext credentials, even in "dev" profiles.
- Reassign the prod port before this and the legacy service are ever deployed together.
- Either remove the Eureka properties or add the actual dependency, but don't leave config that silently does nothing.
- Parameterize file paths (`pdfDir`, `fontDir`, etc.) instead of hardcoding a developer's local path.

